1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,403
Close your eyes and walk into your

2
00:00:02,403 --> 00:00:05,514
family's living room in nineteen sixty-five.

3
00:00:05,694 --> 00:00:07,772
Your feet sink into the carpet.

4
00:00:07,952 --> 00:00:10,178
The television is warming up in the

5
00:00:10,178 --> 00:00:10,623
corner.

6
00:00:10,803 --> 00:00:12,939
Something is ticking on the wall.

7
00:00:13,119 --> 00:00:15,637
There's a smell of furniture polish,

8
00:00:15,637 --> 00:00:18,576
coffee and maybe a little cigarette smoke.

9
00:00:18,756 --> 00:00:21,147
And everything is exactly where it has

10
00:00:21,147 --> 00:00:21,902
always been.

11
00:00:22,252 --> 00:00:24,328
The living room of the sixties was

12
00:00:24,328 --> 00:00:26,222
the heart of the American home.

13
00:00:26,402 --> 00:00:29,005
It was where the family watched television,

14
00:00:29,005 --> 00:00:30,640
where company was received,

15
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:32,154
where Christmas happened,

16
00:00:32,154 --> 00:00:33,909
and where children were told,

17
00:00:33,909 --> 00:00:34,818
more than once,

18
00:00:34,818 --> 00:00:36,513
not to put their feet on the

19
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:37,118
furniture.

20
00:00:37,469 --> 00:00:40,746
So today, let's count down twenty-five things

21
00:00:40,746 --> 00:00:44,097
that almost every nineteen sixties living room

22
00:00:44,097 --> 00:00:44,388
had.

23
00:00:44,568 --> 00:00:46,391
Count how many your family had.

24
00:00:46,571 --> 00:00:48,196
I'll bet it's more than twenty.

25
00:00:48,547 --> 00:00:49,696
And one quick note.

26
00:00:49,876 --> 00:00:50,861
In many homes,

27
00:00:50,861 --> 00:00:53,324
there were really two living rooms.

28
00:00:53,504 --> 00:00:56,368
The everyday room where the family watched

29
00:00:56,368 --> 00:00:58,414
television, and the good room,

30
00:00:58,414 --> 00:00:59,778
or the front parlor,

31
00:00:59,778 --> 00:01:02,029
that was kept perfect for guests,

32
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:04,348
holidays and the minister's visit.

33
00:01:04,528 --> 00:01:07,151
Children were hardly allowed to set foot

34
00:01:07,151 --> 00:01:08,266
in the good room.

35
00:01:08,446 --> 00:01:10,473
You'll see a little of both on

36
00:01:10,473 --> 00:01:11,082
our list.

37
00:01:11,432 --> 00:01:12,778
Number twenty-five.

38
00:01:12,958 --> 00:01:14,944
The plastic slipcovers.

39
00:01:15,124 --> 00:01:16,148
In many homes,

40
00:01:16,148 --> 00:01:18,783
the good couch was covered in clear,

41
00:01:18,783 --> 00:01:20,905
heavy plastic to keep it new.

42
00:01:21,085 --> 00:01:22,862
It squeaked when you sat down.

43
00:01:23,042 --> 00:01:25,345
In summer, it stuck to the backs

44
00:01:25,345 --> 00:01:26,281
of your legs.

45
00:01:26,461 --> 00:01:28,173
And when company came,

46
00:01:28,173 --> 00:01:30,196
the covers might come off,

47
00:01:30,196 --> 00:01:31,831
just for the evening,

48
00:01:31,831 --> 00:01:33,543
then go right back on.

49
00:01:33,893 --> 00:01:35,112
Number twenty-four.

50
00:01:35,292 --> 00:01:36,453
The candy dish.

51
00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:39,243
A glass or cut-crystal dish on the

52
00:01:39,243 --> 00:01:41,930
coffee table, full of ribbon candy,

53
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:46,153
butterscotch disks, or those strawberry candies wrapped

54
00:01:46,153 --> 00:01:48,150
to look like strawberries.

55
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:49,798
At grandma's house,

56
00:01:49,798 --> 00:01:52,658
the candies in the dish had sometimes

57
00:01:52,658 --> 00:01:55,673
fused together into one solid lump that

58
00:01:55,673 --> 00:01:57,374
nobody dared to touch.

59
00:01:57,724 --> 00:01:59,047
Number twenty-three.

60
00:01:59,227 --> 00:02:01,166
The big glass ashtray.

61
00:02:01,346 --> 00:02:02,482
In the sixties,

62
00:02:02,482 --> 00:02:05,059
roughly four in ten adults smoked,

63
00:02:05,059 --> 00:02:07,711
and every living room had ashtrays,

64
00:02:07,711 --> 00:02:10,287
even in homes where nobody smoked,

65
00:02:10,287 --> 00:02:11,423
for the guests.

66
00:02:11,603 --> 00:02:13,910
Heavy glass, amber or green,

67
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,793
sometimes as big as a dinner plate.

68
00:02:16,973 --> 00:02:19,014
And a table lighter shaped like a

69
00:02:19,014 --> 00:02:20,932
little statue or a chess piece.

70
00:02:21,282 --> 00:02:22,489
Number twenty-two.

71
00:02:22,669 --> 00:02:24,190
The console television.

72
00:02:24,370 --> 00:02:25,525
Not a small set,

73
00:02:25,525 --> 00:02:27,330
but a piece of furniture.

74
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:29,072
A long wooden cabinet,

75
00:02:29,072 --> 00:02:31,131
sometimes with sliding doors,

76
00:02:31,131 --> 00:02:33,118
with a screen in the middle.

77
00:02:33,298 --> 00:02:35,334
Through most of the sixties,

78
00:02:35,334 --> 00:02:38,242
many families still watched in black and

79
00:02:38,242 --> 00:02:40,786
white, with three or four channels.

80
00:02:40,966 --> 00:02:42,696
Color sets were expensive,

81
00:02:42,696 --> 00:02:44,426
and they took over slowly.

82
00:02:44,777 --> 00:02:47,371
Turning on that television was a little

83
00:02:47,371 --> 00:02:47,969
ceremony.

84
00:02:48,149 --> 00:02:49,278
You pulled the knob,

85
00:02:49,278 --> 00:02:51,085
and a tiny dot of light appeared

86
00:02:51,085 --> 00:02:52,666
in the middle of the screen.

87
00:02:52,846 --> 00:02:53,957
Then you waited,

88
00:02:53,957 --> 00:02:55,347
maybe half a minute,

89
00:02:55,347 --> 00:02:57,153
while the tubes warmed up.

90
00:02:57,333 --> 00:02:58,308
Late at night,

91
00:02:58,308 --> 00:03:01,095
the station signed off with the national

92
00:03:01,095 --> 00:03:03,602
anthem, and then a test pattern with

93
00:03:03,602 --> 00:03:05,204
an Indian head drawing,

94
00:03:05,204 --> 00:03:05,971
and a long,

95
00:03:05,971 --> 00:03:06,667
high tone.

96
00:03:07,018 --> 00:03:08,132
Number twenty-one.

97
00:03:08,312 --> 00:03:09,508
The rabbit ears.

98
00:03:09,688 --> 00:03:12,055
On top of the television sat an

99
00:03:12,055 --> 00:03:14,576
antenna with two long metal rods.

100
00:03:14,756 --> 00:03:16,399
When the picture got snowy,

101
00:03:16,399 --> 00:03:18,225
someone had to get up and move

102
00:03:18,225 --> 00:03:18,529
them.

103
00:03:18,709 --> 00:03:21,537
Sometimes the best picture came only when

104
00:03:21,537 --> 00:03:23,399
a child stood holding them.

105
00:03:23,579 --> 00:03:26,594
Some families added a piece of aluminum

106
00:03:26,594 --> 00:03:27,908
foil to the tips,

107
00:03:27,908 --> 00:03:29,686
just in case it helped.

108
00:03:30,037 --> 00:03:31,093
Number twenty.

109
00:03:31,273 --> 00:03:33,293
The hi-fi stereo console.

110
00:03:33,473 --> 00:03:35,479
Another long wooden cabinet,

111
00:03:35,479 --> 00:03:38,059
this one with a record player hidden

112
00:03:38,059 --> 00:03:38,918
under a lid,

113
00:03:38,918 --> 00:03:41,426
a radio, and speakers behind cloth.

114
00:03:41,606 --> 00:03:43,444
It played Frank Sinatra,

115
00:03:43,444 --> 00:03:46,507
Herb Alpert, the soundtrack to The Sound

116
00:03:46,507 --> 00:03:49,418
of Music and Christmas albums from the

117
00:03:49,418 --> 00:03:50,337
gas station.

118
00:03:50,517 --> 00:03:52,968
Children were warned never to touch the

119
00:03:52,968 --> 00:03:53,408
needle.

120
00:03:53,758 --> 00:03:55,035
Number nineteen.

121
00:03:55,215 --> 00:03:56,794
Wall-to-wall carpet.

122
00:03:56,974 --> 00:03:58,080
By the sixties,

123
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:01,251
the hardwood floors of earlier decades were

124
00:04:01,251 --> 00:04:02,210
covered over.

125
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:05,711
Shag carpet came later in the decade,

126
00:04:05,711 --> 00:04:07,597
but even before that,

127
00:04:07,597 --> 00:04:10,829
families loved thick carpet in gold,

128
00:04:10,829 --> 00:04:14,151
avocado green, burnt orange or beige.

129
00:04:14,331 --> 00:04:16,961
Some people vacuumed it in a crosshatch

130
00:04:16,961 --> 00:04:18,917
pattern so it looked perfect.

131
00:04:19,267 --> 00:04:20,497
Number eighteen.

132
00:04:20,677 --> 00:04:21,687
The recliner.

133
00:04:21,867 --> 00:04:22,901
Dad's chair.

134
00:04:23,081 --> 00:04:24,941
It might have been a La-Z-Boy,

135
00:04:24,941 --> 00:04:27,360
the brand that made the reclining chair

136
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:27,794
famous.

137
00:04:27,974 --> 00:04:30,065
Nobody else sat in it when he

138
00:04:30,065 --> 00:04:30,714
was home.

139
00:04:30,894 --> 00:04:33,380
Children who did were quickly told to

140
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:33,715
move.

141
00:04:33,895 --> 00:04:35,570
And on Sunday afternoons,

142
00:04:35,570 --> 00:04:37,378
it became a place for a nap

143
00:04:37,378 --> 00:04:39,120
with the football game on.

144
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:41,303
On the arms and the back of

145
00:04:41,303 --> 00:04:43,543
that chair, and on the couch too,

146
00:04:43,543 --> 00:04:44,833
there were doilies.

147
00:04:45,013 --> 00:04:47,480
Little lace or crocheted covers,

148
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:49,638
often made by a grandmother,

149
00:04:49,638 --> 00:04:52,337
to protect the fabric from hair oil

150
00:04:52,337 --> 00:04:53,570
and dirty hands.

151
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:55,930
Men's hair tonic of the time was

152
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:58,860
so greasy that furniture makers sold covers

153
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:00,495
for exactly that reason.

154
00:05:00,675 --> 00:05:03,369
They called them antimacassars.

155
00:05:03,719 --> 00:05:04,961
Number seventeen.

156
00:05:05,141 --> 00:05:06,313
The TV tray.

157
00:05:06,493 --> 00:05:09,390
Folding metal tables with painted flowers or

158
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:11,760
patterns, stored in a little rack in

159
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:12,484
the corner.

160
00:05:12,664 --> 00:05:13,986
On special nights,

161
00:05:13,986 --> 00:05:16,484
the family ate TV dinners off them

162
00:05:16,484 --> 00:05:19,790
while watching Bonanza or The Wonderful World

163
00:05:19,790 --> 00:05:20,524
of Disney.

164
00:05:20,704 --> 00:05:24,172
Swanson TV dinners came in aluminum trays,

165
00:05:24,172 --> 00:05:25,658
with turkey, peas,

166
00:05:25,658 --> 00:05:28,134
potatoes and a little dessert.

167
00:05:28,484 --> 00:05:29,785
Number sixteen.

168
00:05:29,965 --> 00:05:31,103
The TV Guide.

169
00:05:31,283 --> 00:05:33,558
It sat on top of the television,

170
00:05:33,558 --> 00:05:35,193
or on the coffee table,

171
00:05:35,193 --> 00:05:37,041
with that week's listings.

172
00:05:37,221 --> 00:05:40,137
It was the most-read magazine in America

173
00:05:40,137 --> 00:05:41,230
in the sixties,

174
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:43,781
with millions of copies every week.

175
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:46,474
Kids circled the shows they wanted to

176
00:05:46,474 --> 00:05:48,851
see, and the fall preview issue was

177
00:05:48,851 --> 00:05:50,753
a big event every September.

178
00:05:51,102 --> 00:05:52,345
Number fifteen.

179
00:05:52,525 --> 00:05:53,953
The sunburst clock.

180
00:05:54,133 --> 00:05:56,306
A clock on the wall with metal

181
00:05:56,306 --> 00:05:58,985
rays shooting out in every direction,

182
00:05:58,985 --> 00:06:00,216
like a starburst.

183
00:06:00,396 --> 00:06:02,637
It was the height of modern style.

184
00:06:02,817 --> 00:06:04,756
Some had a little electric hum.

185
00:06:04,936 --> 00:06:07,427
Many families also had a wall clock

186
00:06:07,427 --> 00:06:09,063
that chimed every hour,

187
00:06:09,063 --> 00:06:12,194
which visitors loved and children learned to

188
00:06:12,194 --> 00:06:13,191
sleep through.

189
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:14,887
Number fourteen.

190
00:06:15,067 --> 00:06:16,460
The telephone table.

191
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:17,854
Or, in many houses,

192
00:06:17,854 --> 00:06:19,961
the telephone was in the hallway,

193
00:06:19,961 --> 00:06:21,813
on a little table with a seat

194
00:06:21,813 --> 00:06:23,792
and a shelf for the phone book.

195
00:06:23,972 --> 00:06:26,182
The phone was black or beige,

196
00:06:26,182 --> 00:06:27,629
with a rotary dial.

197
00:06:27,809 --> 00:06:29,119
Later in the decade,

198
00:06:29,119 --> 00:06:31,803
there might be a colorful Princess phone.

199
00:06:31,983 --> 00:06:33,709
And there was one phone for the

200
00:06:33,709 --> 00:06:34,433
whole family.

201
00:06:34,782 --> 00:06:36,048
Number thirteen.

202
00:06:36,228 --> 00:06:38,062
The encyclopedias.

203
00:06:38,242 --> 00:06:40,413
A full set of World Book or

204
00:06:40,413 --> 00:06:42,985
Britannica, lined up on a shelf,

205
00:06:42,985 --> 00:06:45,960
sometimes bought one volume at a time

206
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:47,809
from the grocery store,

207
00:06:47,809 --> 00:06:50,220
or from a salesman who came to

208
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:50,944
the door.

209
00:06:51,124 --> 00:06:53,323
Children used them for school reports,

210
00:06:53,323 --> 00:06:55,118
and parents were proud of them.

211
00:06:55,298 --> 00:06:57,374
Many sets ended at the letter S

212
00:06:57,374 --> 00:06:59,651
because the family stopped buying.

213
00:07:00,001 --> 00:07:00,889
Next to them,

214
00:07:00,889 --> 00:07:03,073
there might be a row of Reader's

215
00:07:03,073 --> 00:07:04,644
Digest Condensed Books,

216
00:07:04,644 --> 00:07:05,941
in matching covers,

217
00:07:05,941 --> 00:07:08,193
arriving in the mail four times a

218
00:07:08,193 --> 00:07:08,535
year.

219
00:07:08,715 --> 00:07:11,722
A family photo album with black paper

220
00:07:11,722 --> 00:07:14,079
pages and little corner tabs.

221
00:07:14,259 --> 00:07:16,685
And a big dictionary on a stand.

222
00:07:16,865 --> 00:07:18,150
For many families,

223
00:07:18,150 --> 00:07:20,578
that shelf was their home library,

224
00:07:20,578 --> 00:07:22,648
and it was read over and over

225
00:07:22,648 --> 00:07:23,076
again.

226
00:07:23,426 --> 00:07:24,402
Number twelve.

227
00:07:24,582 --> 00:07:25,789
The family Bible.

228
00:07:25,969 --> 00:07:28,193
A big, heavy book with a soft

229
00:07:28,193 --> 00:07:30,801
leather cover, often on a table of

230
00:07:30,801 --> 00:07:31,414
its own.

231
00:07:31,594 --> 00:07:34,397
Inside the front cover were records of

232
00:07:34,397 --> 00:07:37,053
family births, marriages and deaths,

233
00:07:37,053 --> 00:07:40,151
written in different hands over the years.

234
00:07:40,331 --> 00:07:41,507
In many families,

235
00:07:41,507 --> 00:07:44,135
that Bible is still the most important

236
00:07:44,135 --> 00:07:45,311
record they have.

237
00:07:45,661 --> 00:07:46,567
Number eleven.

238
00:07:46,747 --> 00:07:47,885
The picture window.

239
00:07:48,065 --> 00:07:50,504
Many new houses of the fifties and

240
00:07:50,504 --> 00:07:53,301
sixties had a big picture window facing

241
00:07:53,301 --> 00:07:54,090
the street.

242
00:07:54,270 --> 00:07:55,261
It let in light,

243
00:07:55,261 --> 00:07:57,305
and showed off the living room to

244
00:07:57,305 --> 00:07:58,171
the neighbors.

245
00:07:58,351 --> 00:07:59,737
And in front of it,

246
00:07:59,737 --> 00:08:01,852
families often placed a lamp,

247
00:08:01,852 --> 00:08:03,311
a plant or a statue,

248
00:08:03,311 --> 00:08:05,863
so it looked nice from the outside.

249
00:08:06,213 --> 00:08:07,223
Number ten.

250
00:08:07,403 --> 00:08:08,622
The swag lamp.

251
00:08:08,802 --> 00:08:10,523
A hanging lamp on a chain,

252
00:08:10,523 --> 00:08:13,303
looped across the ceiling and plugged into

253
00:08:13,303 --> 00:08:13,898
the wall.

254
00:08:14,078 --> 00:08:16,967
Some had glass globes in amber or

255
00:08:16,967 --> 00:08:17,318
red.

256
00:08:17,498 --> 00:08:19,423
Others had a lamp on the end

257
00:08:19,423 --> 00:08:22,173
table with a pleated shade still wrapped

258
00:08:22,173 --> 00:08:23,616
in its plastic cover,

259
00:08:23,616 --> 00:08:25,404
years after it was bought.

260
00:08:25,754 --> 00:08:26,671
Number nine.

261
00:08:26,851 --> 00:08:28,464
The family portraits.

262
00:08:28,644 --> 00:08:29,568
On the wall,

263
00:08:29,568 --> 00:08:31,722
or on top of the television,

264
00:08:31,722 --> 00:08:33,799
there were framed pictures.

265
00:08:33,979 --> 00:08:35,152
Wedding photos.

266
00:08:35,332 --> 00:08:37,787
School pictures of every child,

267
00:08:37,787 --> 00:08:39,291
updated every year,

268
00:08:39,291 --> 00:08:41,508
with the same awkward smile.

269
00:08:41,688 --> 00:08:44,266
A photo of a son in uniform.

270
00:08:44,446 --> 00:08:47,069
And the formal family portrait from the

271
00:08:47,069 --> 00:08:48,684
department store studio,

272
00:08:48,684 --> 00:08:50,971
with everyone in matching outfits.

273
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:52,203
Number eight.

274
00:08:52,383 --> 00:08:53,393
The houseplants.

275
00:08:53,573 --> 00:08:56,330
A spider plant hanging in a macrame

276
00:08:56,330 --> 00:08:56,882
holder.

277
00:08:57,062 --> 00:08:58,826
A rubber tree in the corner.

278
00:08:59,006 --> 00:09:01,491
African violets on the windowsill.

279
00:09:01,671 --> 00:09:03,778
And sometimes plastic flowers,

280
00:09:03,778 --> 00:09:06,867
which were very fashionable and never needed

281
00:09:06,867 --> 00:09:07,499
watering.

282
00:09:07,679 --> 00:09:09,419
The dust collected on them,

283
00:09:09,419 --> 00:09:11,288
but that was a small price to

284
00:09:11,288 --> 00:09:11,545
pay.

285
00:09:11,896 --> 00:09:12,952
Number seven.

286
00:09:13,132 --> 00:09:14,281
The coffee table.

287
00:09:14,461 --> 00:09:15,472
Long and low,

288
00:09:15,472 --> 00:09:16,483
made of wood,

289
00:09:16,483 --> 00:09:18,583
sometimes with a glass top.

290
00:09:18,763 --> 00:09:20,690
On it sat the candy dish,

291
00:09:20,690 --> 00:09:23,695
the ashtray, a few magazines like Life,

292
00:09:23,695 --> 00:09:27,240
Look, Reader's Digest and The Saturday Evening

293
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:29,860
Post, and maybe a big coffee table

294
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:32,172
book about the national parks.

295
00:09:32,522 --> 00:09:33,672
Number six.

296
00:09:33,852 --> 00:09:35,059
The knick-knack shelf.

297
00:09:35,239 --> 00:09:37,752
A small wall shelf or a glass

298
00:09:37,752 --> 00:09:41,044
cabinet, filled with little figurines.

299
00:09:41,224 --> 00:09:43,943
Hummel children, porcelain birds,

300
00:09:43,943 --> 00:09:47,074
salt and pepper shakers from vacation,

301
00:09:47,074 --> 00:09:48,721
a tiny Eiffel Tower,

302
00:09:48,721 --> 00:09:51,522
souvenir spoons and a ceramic dog.

303
00:09:51,702 --> 00:09:53,662
Everything was dusted every week,

304
00:09:53,662 --> 00:09:55,859
and children were not allowed to play

305
00:09:55,859 --> 00:09:56,869
anywhere near it.

306
00:09:57,219 --> 00:09:58,275
Number five.

307
00:09:58,455 --> 00:10:00,278
The piano or organ.

308
00:10:00,458 --> 00:10:03,091
Many families had an upright piano,

309
00:10:03,091 --> 00:10:05,800
often handed down from grandparents,

310
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:08,283
and children took weekly lessons.

311
00:10:08,463 --> 00:10:09,520
In the sixties,

312
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:12,551
home organs from companies like Hammond and

313
00:10:12,551 --> 00:10:14,384
Lowrey became popular too.

314
00:10:14,564 --> 00:10:17,958
Every Christmas, someone played carols while the

315
00:10:17,958 --> 00:10:19,231
whole family sang.

316
00:10:19,581 --> 00:10:20,995
And next to the piano,

317
00:10:20,995 --> 00:10:22,472
often, a sewing basket.

318
00:10:22,652 --> 00:10:25,773
Mothers and grandmothers knitted and crocheted in

319
00:10:25,773 --> 00:10:27,684
the living room every evening,

320
00:10:27,684 --> 00:10:29,467
while the television played.

321
00:10:29,647 --> 00:10:31,530
Afghan blankets in orange,

322
00:10:31,530 --> 00:10:34,498
brown and yellow squares were draped over

323
00:10:34,498 --> 00:10:36,091
the back of the couch.

324
00:10:36,271 --> 00:10:38,870
Almost every family had at least one,

325
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:40,555
and many still do today.

326
00:10:40,904 --> 00:10:41,845
Number four.

327
00:10:42,025 --> 00:10:44,440
The bowling trophies and the souvenirs.

328
00:10:44,620 --> 00:10:45,396
On a shelf,

329
00:10:45,396 --> 00:10:47,372
or on top of the television,

330
00:10:47,372 --> 00:10:50,477
there were trophies from the bowling league,

331
00:10:50,477 --> 00:10:53,300
the Little League or the church softball

332
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:53,652
team.

333
00:10:53,832 --> 00:10:54,956
And next to them,

334
00:10:54,956 --> 00:10:57,269
souvenirs from the family vacation.

335
00:10:57,449 --> 00:10:59,306
A plate from Niagara Falls,

336
00:10:59,306 --> 00:11:01,508
a pennant from the Grand Canyon,

337
00:11:01,508 --> 00:11:03,916
and a little bear from Yellowstone.

338
00:11:04,265 --> 00:11:05,299
Number three.

339
00:11:05,479 --> 00:11:07,963
The bar cart or the liquor cabinet.

340
00:11:08,143 --> 00:11:10,789
Many living rooms of the sixties had

341
00:11:10,789 --> 00:11:11,745
a little bar,

342
00:11:11,745 --> 00:11:13,362
with highball glasses,

343
00:11:13,362 --> 00:11:14,391
an ice bucket,

344
00:11:14,391 --> 00:11:17,331
a cocktail shaker and bottles of whiskey

345
00:11:17,331 --> 00:11:17,919
and gin.

346
00:11:18,099 --> 00:11:20,784
When friends came over on a Saturday

347
00:11:20,784 --> 00:11:23,544
night, Dad mixed martinis and Mom put

348
00:11:23,544 --> 00:11:26,080
out bowls of mixed nuts and cheese

349
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:26,528
balls.

350
00:11:26,878 --> 00:11:27,841
Number two.

351
00:11:28,021 --> 00:11:29,739
The Christmas tree spot.

352
00:11:29,919 --> 00:11:32,725
Every living room had one corner that,

353
00:11:32,725 --> 00:11:33,611
once a year,

354
00:11:33,611 --> 00:11:36,268
became the most magical place in the

355
00:11:36,268 --> 00:11:36,711
house.

356
00:11:36,891 --> 00:11:37,932
In the sixties,

357
00:11:37,932 --> 00:11:39,735
it might hold a real tree,

358
00:11:39,735 --> 00:11:42,717
or a brand-new aluminum tree that glittered

359
00:11:42,717 --> 00:11:44,728
under a rotating color wheel.

360
00:11:44,908 --> 00:11:48,046
Bubble lights, tinsel and big colored bulbs

361
00:11:48,046 --> 00:11:50,016
filled the room with light.

362
00:11:50,366 --> 00:11:51,597
And number one.

363
00:11:51,777 --> 00:11:53,226
The family itself,

364
00:11:53,226 --> 00:11:55,318
all together, in one room.

365
00:11:55,498 --> 00:11:57,725
Before every child had a screen of

366
00:11:57,725 --> 00:12:00,084
their own, the living room was where

367
00:12:00,084 --> 00:12:02,114
everyone ended up at the end of

368
00:12:02,114 --> 00:12:02,638
the day.

369
00:12:02,818 --> 00:12:04,099
Dad in his recliner,

370
00:12:04,099 --> 00:12:06,340
Mom on the couch with her knitting,

371
00:12:06,340 --> 00:12:08,133
and the kids on the floor in

372
00:12:08,133 --> 00:12:09,670
front of the television,

373
00:12:09,670 --> 00:12:11,911
close enough to get a lecture about

374
00:12:11,911 --> 00:12:13,128
ruining their eyes.

375
00:12:13,478 --> 00:12:15,916
So why do we remember those living

376
00:12:15,916 --> 00:12:17,135
rooms so clearly?

377
00:12:17,315 --> 00:12:18,940
Because nothing ever moved.

378
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:20,222
The same couch,

379
00:12:20,222 --> 00:12:21,251
the same lamp,

380
00:12:21,251 --> 00:12:23,601
the same clock for twenty years.

381
00:12:23,781 --> 00:12:26,614
Families didn't redecorate every few years.

382
00:12:26,794 --> 00:12:28,781
Furniture was bought to last,

383
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:29,534
and it did.

384
00:12:29,884 --> 00:12:33,112
And because those rooms held our childhoods.

385
00:12:33,292 --> 00:12:34,924
The Christmas mornings,

386
00:12:34,924 --> 00:12:37,690
the Sunday nights watching Ed Sullivan,

387
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:40,528
the moon landing in nineteen sixty-nine,

388
00:12:40,528 --> 00:12:42,798
when the whole family sat on the

389
00:12:42,798 --> 00:12:44,856
floor and watched in silence.

390
00:12:45,036 --> 00:12:46,301
We didn't know it then,

391
00:12:46,301 --> 00:12:48,282
but every evening there was a memory

392
00:12:48,282 --> 00:12:49,053
in the making.

393
00:12:49,402 --> 00:12:51,353
So tell us in the comments.

394
00:12:51,533 --> 00:12:53,757
How many of these twenty-five did your

395
00:12:53,757 --> 00:12:54,459
family have?

396
00:12:54,639 --> 00:12:56,385
And what's one thing in your living

397
00:12:56,385 --> 00:12:57,483
room I didn't mention?

398
00:12:57,663 --> 00:12:59,149
We read every comment.

399
00:12:59,329 --> 00:13:02,002
Next time, we're going back to growing

400
00:13:02,002 --> 00:13:03,901
up in nineteen seventy-two,

401
00:13:03,901 --> 00:13:05,378
the forgotten decade.

402
00:13:05,558 --> 00:13:07,405
Thank you for spending this time with

403
00:13:07,405 --> 00:13:07,555
me.
